#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228844 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd54adb4-ef2b-5f9d-b920-4ec460bcd199" :question/math-academy-id "e-21611" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' + y = -10.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ to this equation." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny'_c + y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y_c}{\\textrm{d}x} = -y_c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y_c} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y_c} {\\textrm{d}x} = -1\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make $y_c$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K= e^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A = \\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228845 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0e14ce3-61de-571e-b34e-f56630cc2fe9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21612" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} +  y = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\alpha x+\\beta.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta) + (\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha x^2 + (2\\alpha+ \\beta)x + (\\beta+ \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=1, \\beta=0, \\gamma=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = x ^ 2 - 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228846 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "344fb3a9-81db-5744-822c-1f989c0cc4de" :question/math-academy-id "e-21613" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = -2x + 5.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -2x + 5.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x + (\\alpha +2\\beta) = -2x + 5.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-1, \\,\\beta=3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = -x + 3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{-2x} - x + 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228847 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f89b7459-5b3d-5da3-a76a-7df3378cb048" :question/math-academy-id "e-21614" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' + 4y = -8x + 6, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 4y = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{-4x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 4y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha + 4(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha x + (\\alpha + 4\\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +4\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-2, \\,\\beta = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)= -2x + 2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-4x} -2x + 2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) = 1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-4(0)}-2(0)+2 & =1 \\\\ A+2 & =1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny \n=  -e^{-4x} - 2x + 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2015e534-fba6-51c1-a9d5-9ae2b3892120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301208} {:answer/id #uuid "ce55c6ac-cee0-5916-b7ad-2ef46a12a4de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301209} {:answer/id #uuid "73770f48-2dfb-5828-95b6-c6f72b8882dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301210} {:answer/id #uuid "be564220-7731-5480-b9e5-fa73ceef3eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301211} {:answer/id #uuid "1c58247e-7981-56da-8b5e-47ae4ce2630a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2015e534-fba6-51c1-a9d5-9ae2b3892120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301208} :answer-field/id #uuid "a954b816-d048-57b5-afe1-dd002c9cd01f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "279c6e70-99fa-5736-91b3-186d8e98a23c" :question/math-academy-id "q-331877" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=3{x}^{2}-2x+5.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{4x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55ab4aa1-0dbe-52d4-8331-29491c0009fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301215} {:answer/id #uuid "b3234d59-b988-5b25-98fc-55cbdac73e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-4x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301216} {:answer/id #uuid "669440ea-5538-5ea8-99c9-0fb8326df3e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-4x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301217} {:answer/id #uuid "72541d41-5ad8-522a-a4ab-b75179903f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301218} {:answer/id #uuid "0f874757-e58d-5f3a-969b-2de0aa96f4b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55ab4aa1-0dbe-52d4-8331-29491c0009fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301215} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec8a9c39-5604-5b2d-b090-9fee7d8b9984" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d9cadaa1-0348-5108-b0ed-d95da25f0147" :question/math-academy-id "q-331920" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=4{x}^{2}-4x+1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\beta -2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha -2\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta -2\\gamma =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =-\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91d1688a-e8ff-523e-bc5e-197a45513b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91d1688a-e8ff-523e-bc5e-197a45513b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238166} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d7a430-dcba-5ba3-91bb-ccf7a6e07e80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fc5d25c-b291-5d77-bf05-80ed6712a38b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332265" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=12.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =12\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa703ac5-040e-530e-8fc6-2d203511a822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa703ac5-040e-530e-8fc6-2d203511a822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301222} :answer-field/id #uuid "38b7a90c-bb3e-54d0-ba39-8414e6256c52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b28d5fe7-c6cd-5018-9178-d5a601b91e1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-332289" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=15.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =15\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "805fa3fb-59af-5323-aae2-cfa62895ebf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301225} {:answer/id #uuid "a02a2559-6128-5c31-8b49-279e748d4e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a02a2559-6128-5c31-8b49-279e748d4e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301226} :answer-field/id #uuid "37346fd5-4175-5566-9ae4-0e53ec30c5df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e254e74-aca3-5cd2-a47c-6d3276e604d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332295" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=-20.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha =-20\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef49e4d0-03df-5674-8144-b982a7a45316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301229} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7d4a37-ff77-5e7c-8b1e-63a23a918bd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef49e4d0-03df-5674-8144-b982a7a45316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301229} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed3ae9e1-2798-53dc-a38f-5465ec956d54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "069198d4-e282-5b46-ba93-6cbba62d1687" :question/math-academy-id "q-332302" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=8x+9.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +8(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x+9\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n8\\alpha x+(\\alpha +8\\beta )=8x+9.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +8\\beta =9\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af4129b6-f61c-5770-bf82-d79508231cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301233} {:answer/id #uuid "62f261c2-cd14-58c0-80df-ddeb79a72f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301234} {:answer/id #uuid "31a0a489-a3c9-5a77-8220-5ef43af0f206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{2}+2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301235} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f28f54-c8e7-5d9d-9244-9b01ed1a03d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301236} {:answer/id #uuid "da18f759-ff18-5864-9fc5-f37402153961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af4129b6-f61c-5770-bf82-d79508231cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301233} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef720cc0-1e9e-5d72-af64-8ca46565f4fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0059abd6-dc4c-559c-9481-34b1f39c6f24" :question/math-academy-id "q-332312" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a145f527-5c8e-5e9c-8126-676a9617c31d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{3x}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301240} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fb5d74-8054-59d8-a0aa-af0380bf1c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{3x}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4fb5d74-8054-59d8-a0aa-af0380bf1c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{3x}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301241} :answer-field/id #uuid "44504acb-40f3-5cc4-ad0e-151cd18d2e33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc235b3a-1731-5566-a624-628471650abf" :question/math-academy-id "q-332376" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=9x-3.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=9x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\alpha x+(\\alpha -3\\beta )=9x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha =9\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -3\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d09639d4-7ca7-5167-96d5-59ed6c4007c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-3x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d09639d4-7ca7-5167-96d5-59ed6c4007c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-3x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238170} :answer-field/id #uuid "185433c7-11c2-55a1-add6-78877678c714" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9d8bb74-de23-5617-b920-d92d442130c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-332543" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=6.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+3\\beta =6\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b7489ad-303a-579f-8d97-f60645cc3f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-6x}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b7489ad-303a-579f-8d97-f60645cc3f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-6x}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301244} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e0f49b-23d4-525a-8baa-9e59b924e623" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0437cac1-0430-51b8-a73f-f9f901b83c52" :question/math-academy-id "q-332547" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-30.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 0+6\\beta =-30\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x}-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c9118bc-ed65-5ad8-905d-c0def882717f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-4x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301247} {:answer/id #uuid "75e06894-732c-54e4-b86f-e8d71ccb76ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-4x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301248} {:answer/id #uuid "8195fd33-4ab7-5e3b-84f6-7ede3d063976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-{x}^{2}-4x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301249} {:answer/id #uuid "7ece337a-30e2-5059-a6a1-fdab3768f67b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+2{x}^{2}-4x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301250} {:answer/id #uuid "1318eef8-93f7-570e-aa25-456973883908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c9118bc-ed65-5ad8-905d-c0def882717f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-4x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301247} :answer-field/id #uuid "595bef39-61a0-5f5b-9dd1-0332bad33c71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ce3e0989-e7de-5d8b-bc2e-aea033858039" :question/math-academy-id "q-332556" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=-{x}^{2}+6x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+6x-7\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -\\beta )x+(\\beta -\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+6x-7\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta -\\gamma =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =-4,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}-4x+3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-4x+3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "946a58f0-7fda-591a-962b-a3c3f4426cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A\\ln (7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228057} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5a9ea0-5527-5929-8d01-79dc82bf0f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228058} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b45141-c649-5a43-ba1c-3e06ed33f83c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{14x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228059} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad7c19f-65c4-5588-9ddf-a2033b23ee38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228060} {:answer/id #uuid "661f729b-bf31-590c-ab1a-051603cfc5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-14x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ad7c19f-65c4-5588-9ddf-a2033b23ee38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228060} :answer-field/id #uuid "c491bc83-cb48-5ee1-af92-a1ed74a9acd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "562b8019-27f2-5021-a398-19bcd11af241" :question/math-academy-id "q-332701" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7y=3x+4.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-7{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-7\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -7\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -7\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-7x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-7x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-7x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-7x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b02d7633-1a0c-54cb-beff-2b6707e1f6de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301254} {:answer/id #uuid "1a2bf25c-f2b3-5734-b888-15d2f3e113a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301255} {:answer/id #uuid "80af1a97-c5fb-55e9-85ee-cf2f7163a877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301256} {:answer/id #uuid "a85d9de8-36cf-5344-bd94-8bd4c73813e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301257} {:answer/id #uuid "7e925c98-38b1-58d1-83f6-92c12bb9469d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80af1a97-c5fb-55e9-85ee-cf2f7163a877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301256} :answer-field/id #uuid "66611d77-d5e4-54bb-935b-01e52e22c116" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b716f9d-06f2-5b91-b42d-dc103f036078" :question/math-academy-id "q-332704" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-x+6.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{4x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d539de16-5135-5bb9-a8c2-da4a0ba70dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{18x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301261} {:answer/id #uuid "54095541-0443-5cb3-bb1a-87635bc7